        "kind": "history",
        "musicCue": "drop",
        "lines": [
          {
            "id": "l3",
            "text": "Two thousand years ago, Romans wrote et, Latin for and.",
            "emphasis": [
              "et"
            ],
            "claimIds": [
              "c1"
            ],
            "pauseAfterSec": 0.2
          },
          {
            "id": "l4",
            "text": "Writing fast, they joined the E and the T into one shape.",
            "emphasis": [
              "joined"
            ],
            "claimIds": [
              "c1"
            ],
            "pauseAfterSec": 0.2
          },
          {
            "id": "l5",
            "text": "Look closely and you can still see them.",
            "emphasis": [
              "see"
            ],
            "claimIds": [
              "c1"
            ],
            "pauseAfterSec": 0.3
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "b3",
        "kind": "mechanism",
        "musicCue": "none",
        "lines": [
          {
            "id": "l6",
            "text": "Centuries later, children learned it as the last letter of the alphabet.",
            "emphasis": [
              "last"
            ],
            "claimIds": [
              "c2"
            ],
            "pauseAfterSec": 0.2
          },
          {
            "id": "l7",
            "text": "After Z, they'd say: and per se and.",
            "emphasis": [
              "per"
            ],
            "claimIds": [
              "c3"
            ],
            "pauseAfterSec": 0.25
          },
          {
            "id": "l8",
            "text": "Meaning: this symbol by itself means and.",
            "emphasis": [
              "itself"
            ],
            "claimIds": [
              "c3"
            ],
            "pauseAfterSec": 0.3
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "b4",
        "kind": "twist",
        "musicCue": "none",
        "lines": [
          {
            "id": "l9",
            "text": "Say it fast enough, and per se and becomes ampersand.",
            "emphasis": [
              "ampersand"
            ],
            "claimIds": [
              "c3"
            ],
            "pauseAfterSec": 0.25
          },
          {
            "id": "l10",
            "text": "By 1837, the slurred name was common English.",
            "emphasis": [
              "1837"
            ],
            "claimIds": [
              "c4"
            ],
            "pauseAfterSec": 0.3
          },
          {
            "id": "l11",
            "text": "And no, it wasn't named after the physicist Ampère.",
            "emphasis": [
              "Ampère"
            ],
            "claimIds": [
              "c5"
            ],
            "pauseAfterSec": 0.2
          },
          {
            "id": "l12",
            "text": "That's a myth.",
            "emphasis": [
              "myth"
            ],
            "claimIds": [
              "c5"
            ],
            "pauseAfterSec": 0.35
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "b5",
        "kind": "payoff",
        "musicCue": "none",
        "lines": [
          {
            "id": "l13",
            "text": "So the name is a mispronunciation, and the symbol is a Roman shortcut.",
            "emphasis": [
              "shortcut"
